### What is the Command pattern for undo/redo?

A command interface declares `execute()` and `undo()`. Each concrete command captures its receiver and parameters, making it a self-contained unit of work. An invoker runs commands and keeps a history stack for undo and a redo stack for redo.

```java
package com.bank.command;

public interface BankCommand {
    void execute();
    void undo();
    String describe();
}
```

### How do you implement transfer commands?

Each command holds the account and amount. Execute performs the action, undo performs the reverse.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferCommand implements BankCommand {
    private final Account from;
    private final Account to;
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    public TransferCommand(Account from, Account to, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.from = from;
        this.to = to;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() {
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
        System.out.println("Transferred INR " + amount
            + " from " + from.getId() + " to " + to.getId());
    }

    @Override
    public void undo() {
        to.debit(amount);
        from.credit(amount);
        System.out.println("Reversed transfer of INR " + amount);
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return "Transfer INR " + amount + " from "
             + from.getId() + " to " + to.getId();
    }
}
```

### How does the invoker manage undo and redo?

The invoker keeps a history stack and a redo stack. Execute pushes to history and clears redo. Undo pops from history, calls undo, and pushes to redo. Redo pops from redo, calls execute, and pushes to history.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class BankInvoker {
    private final Deque<BankCommand> history = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<BankCommand> redoStack = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void execute(BankCommand cmd) {
        cmd.execute();
        history.push(cmd);
        redoStack.clear();
    }

    public void undo() {
        if (history.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Nothing to undo");
            return;
        }
        BankCommand cmd = history.pop();
        cmd.undo();
        redoStack.push(cmd);
    }

    public void redo() {
        if (redoStack.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Nothing to redo");
            return;
        }
        BankCommand cmd = redoStack.pop();
        cmd.execute();
        history.push(cmd);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.command.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CommandDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account rajesh = new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        Account priya = new Account("SAV3002", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        BankInvoker invoker = new BankInvoker();

        invoker.execute(new TransferCommand(
            rajesh, priya, new BigDecimal("25000")));
        System.out.println("Rajesh: " + rajesh.getBalance());
        System.out.println("Priya: " + priya.getBalance());

        invoker.undo();
        System.out.println("After undo - Rajesh: " + rajesh.getBalance());
        System.out.println("After undo - Priya: " + priya.getBalance());

        invoker.redo();
        System.out.println("After redo - Rajesh: " + rajesh.getBalance());
    }
}
```

```text
Transferred INR 25000 from SAV2001 to SAV3002
Rajesh: 75000
Priya: 75000
Reversed transfer of INR 25000
After undo - Rajesh: 100000
After undo - Priya: 50000
Transferred INR 25000 from SAV2001 to SAV3002
After redo - Rajesh: 75000
```

### How do you queue commands for batch execution?

Because commands are objects, they can be enqueued and processed later.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class BatchQueue {
    private final Queue<BankCommand> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    public void enqueue(BankCommand cmd) {
        System.out.println("Queued: " + cmd.describe());
        queue.add(cmd);
    }

    public void processAll() {
        System.out.println("Processing batch of " + queue.size() + " commands");
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            queue.poll().execute();
        }
    }
}
```

```java
BatchQueue batch = new BatchQueue();
batch.enqueue(new TransferCommand(rajesh, priya, new BigDecimal("10000")));
batch.enqueue(new TransferCommand(priya, rajesh, new BigDecimal("5000")));
batch.processAll();
```

```text
Queued: Transfer INR 10000 from SAV2001 to SAV3002
Queued: Transfer INR 5000 from SAV3002 to SAV2001
Processing batch of 2 commands
Transferred INR 10000 from SAV2001 to SAV3002
Transferred INR 5000 from SAV3002 to SAV2001
```

### How do you log commands for audit?

Each command's `describe()` method provides a human-readable representation for the audit log.

```java
public void execute(BankCommand cmd) {
    auditLog.info("EXECUTING: " + cmd.describe());
    cmd.execute();
    history.push(cmd);
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is not capturing enough state for undo. A transfer must store both accounts and the amount.

The second mistake is allowing double-execution or double-undo, which corrupts balances.

```java
// Wrong: no guard against double execution
cmd.execute(); // first time: correct
cmd.execute(); // second time: double debit

// Correct: track execution state
private boolean executed = false;
public void execute() {
    if (executed) throw new IllegalStateException("Already executed");
    from.debit(amount);
    to.credit(amount);
    executed = true;
}
```

### When should you use the Command pattern?

Use it when you need undo/redo, batch processing, audit logging, or operation composition. Do not over-engineer simple operations that do not need reversal.

```java
// Good use case: bank transfer that needs undo and audit
BankCommand cmd = new TransferCommand(rajesh, priya, amount);
invoker.execute(cmd);

// Over-engineering: simple read-only query
BankCommand cmd = new GetBalanceCommand(rajesh); // no undo needed
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The crucial detail for undo is that each command must capture enough state to reverse itself exactly. For a transfer, that means the amount and both accounts. Pankaj keeps commands immutable and self-contained so they are safe to queue and replay across threads. In modern Java, a simple fire-and-forget action is just a `Runnable`; the Command pattern earns its weight when undo, audit, or composition is needed.

```java
// Simple case: Runnable is enough
executor.submit(() -> account.debit(amount));

// Complex case: full Command for undo + audit
invoker.execute(new TransferCommand(from, to, amount));
```
